{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically","id":1511471,"title":"Dark Ages: customising weapons to superhuman strength realistically","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-12T12:50:28-07:00","id":1511471,"title":"Dark Ages: customising weapons to superhuman strength realistically","content":"This question is less about game lore or mechanics, and more about actual weapon crafting.<br /> <br /> I know that in the real world one can - and would, if professional - customise the draw weight of their bow to match their strength. <br /> When dealing with characters with Strength + Potence 6+, is it still possible to produce bows with a draw weight suited to them, for more damage?<br /> In a dark age setting, would there be materials available to make composite bows for them? I have been told that the shorter the arms of the bow, the greater the draw weight, so size would not seem to be a problem for superhuman bows.<br /> <br /> What about thrown weapons like a javelin or hatchet? Would increases in weight or size and minimum Strength required mean greater damage? How much is too much to be realistic? I'm assuming weight can only be increased so much before one has to also increase the weapon's size, and wielding giant javelins, even if possible Strength-wise, would not seem realistic in the game, because the size would make movement inconvenient.<br /> <br /> Similar question for hand-to-hand weapons: what can you increase and how much before the size of the weapons makes it inconvenient to move? My character (currently Str 2 and Potence 4) currently wields a great sword using just one hand, but I might want something customised to my strength when it increases.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T15:15:55-07:00","id":1511480,"title":"","content":"perhaps if you somehow knew how to take advantage of the complicated moving parts found in modern sportsbows, but failing that, you're probably not going to get much more powerful than a modern 7.62x39 round with anything you could pass off as reasonable, if the numbers I'm getting looking at a roman balista are anything to go by. Late medieval crossbows could also put out similar numbers but they lost out to guns for a lot of reasons. Presumably, someone so strong could hand-pull a windlass crossbow, but you're really getting deminishing returns for your strength. <br /> <br /> <br /> I don't think Potence applies to throwing weapons, and I think the issue here is also velocity. Excess potence won't make your arm move faster, it might reduce the resistance of heavy objects but there's still a hard cap on your speed. And no, Celerity doesn't fix things, it's not true acceleration in the F=M/A kinda sense, Celerity is wibbly wobbly time magic and acts as a <i>fast forward</i> , or at least everything you throw using celerity loses it's magical speed after it leaves your hand. <br /> <br /> Potence will let you throw a really heavy thing as if it's light. Maybe Iron wardarts with a lead core for more weight might suit you. <br /> <br /> Giving yourself some kind of ultra-big warhammer doesn't make sense with how I imagine Potence. Potence is probably more efficient than adding more weight to a hammer. Swinging a small hammer easily through someone is going to be better than fighting a large hammer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T15:43:50-07:00","id":1511481,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511480#post1511480\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think Potence applies to throwing weapons, and I think the issue here is also velocity. Excess potence won't make your arm move faster, it might reduce the resistance of heavy objects but there's still a hard cap on your speed. And no, Celerity doesn't fix things, it's not true acceleration in the F=M/A kinda sense, Celerity is wibbly wobbly time magic and acts as a <i>fast forward</i> , or at least everything you throw using celerity loses it's magical speed after it leaves your hand.<br /> <br /> Potence will let you throw a really heavy thing as if it's light. Maybe Iron wardarts with a lead core for more weight might suit you.</div> </div> </div> </div>In VDA20, what we're using to play, the physical disciplines are straightforward: they just constantly and passively add dice to the corresponding physical attribute (plus some special effects if you spend 1 BP). Therefore in my playing group, and that's how I understand the rules to be in the core manual anyway, not just for my group as a houserule, having Str 3 and Potence 3 is the same as having Str 6, except for the fact that you may not look bulky as you would expect, and you may display greater strength for a turn by spending 1 BP to transform the extra dice of Potence into automatic successes (which doesn't affect weapons strength requirements, though it obvious affects damage).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T15:58:36-07:00","id":1511482,"title":"","content":"Sufficiently big and heavy weapons would throw off the wielder's balance with every swing or even just lifting it, regardless of how supernaturally strong they are. So whether it's melee or thrown, a weapon can only get so large on a human frame. You'd need to be physically larger (and thus heavier) for it to be practical. (Ex: A giant Bygone, like from <i>Bygone Bestiary</i>. But that raises unrelated complications.)<br /> <br /> Weapons made of Lead, or possibly like Tungston(?), might be the most effective force multiplier. And only if they're regular sized weapons. For a Vampire with Potence 6+, it's the weapon arm, not the weapon, that's the real driving force here (no pun intended).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T16:16:20-07:00","id":1511485,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511480#post1511480\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">perhaps if you somehow knew how to take advantage of the complicated moving parts found in modern sportsbows, but failing that, you're probably not going to get much more powerful than a modern 7.62x39 round with anything you could pass off as reasonable, if the numbers I'm getting looking at a roman balista are anything to go by. Late medieval crossbows could also put out similar numbers but they lost out to guns for a lot of reasons. Presumably, someone so strong could hand-pull a windlass crossbow, but you're really getting deminishing returns for your strength.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can imagine the need for more modern techniques and materials for very high levels of strength - 8+ perhaps? -, but bows have been made with varying draw weights since ancient times, both for practice and actual daily usage. Stories (or legends, which is good enough for the game) of very hard to draw and very powerful bows are not hard to find. I assume no bows requiring more than Str 5 would be known simply because no human could pull them, but I think the materials and crafting techniques used to increase draw weight could still be tweaked until a Str 7 perhaps. In VDA20 the short bow requires Min Str 2 or 3 (can't remember) and does 2 dice of damage; a long bow requires Str 4 and does 4 dice. I think they wrote bows in a bit of a limited way, precisely because they did not account for the fact that bows were built for different strengths, so one could have a short bow designed for a Str 5 archer that would be more damaging. But then, it's a game and weaponscrafting is not the game's focus, or else we would need rules for that. But then again, there are weirdos like me who do want rules for that... lol<br /> <br /> I don't honestly know the maximum draw weight one can obtain with a modern compound bow vs a premodern composite one. From my digging so far I think I've understood one advantage of modern compound bows is that a single bow can be regulated for different draw weights by turning certain keys, which makes it easier for gradual increase in the archer's strength as opposed to what was done in premodern times, giving the trainee different gradually heavier practice bows each with a fixed draw weight. This advantage is not essential to my query in game though, because I'm happy getting customised bows built for characters.<br /> <br /> <br /> QUOTE=MyWifeIsScary;n1511480]Giving yourself some kind of ultra-big warhammer doesn't make sense with how I imagine Potence. Potence is probably more efficient than adding more weight to a hammer. Swinging a small hammer easily through someone is going to be better than fighting a large hammer. [/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> More Potence in VDA20 always means more damage, naturally, regardless of what weapon you wield. However, a sabre requires Str 2 and does Str (which is inclusive of Potence in this edition) +2 dice of damage; a greatsword requires Str 4 and two hands, or Str 6 and one hand and deals Str + 4. Basically size and weight matter, as they add more dice to the damage, on top of what Str and Potence offer. So if a character has, say, Str+Potence=8, it's legitimate to wonder: can I make a weapon that requires Min Str 8 to be wielded without penalties, but deals Str + Potence + 8 dice of damage, i.e. 16 dice, instead of just using a greatsword and dealing 4 less dice of damage?<br /> <br /> Having said that, some digging showed me that the heaviest swords, zweihanders, weighed around 2 kg (one hand sword were around 1 kg). Heavier swords, up to 9kg, are seen in some museum but they were ceremonial, not for real use in battle, because they were too heavy to be effective: swing after swing, a heavy weapon tires the arm of normal humans.<br /> A vampire with 6+ Str obviously can swing more weight without problem. If the zweihander/greatsword weighs 2 kg, requires Min Str 4 and 2 hands (or Str 6 one-handed) and deals +4 dice of damage, and a sabre weighs 1 kg and deals +2, one could rule that for game purposes a sword requiring Min Str 6 two-handed or 8 one-handed would deal +6 dice of damage and weigh around 3 kg. Basically add 1kg to the weight every 2 dots of Min Str required and keep the damage equal to the Min Str. Following this, a sword for a Brujah Mathusela with Str 9 and Potence 9 would weigh just 9 kg. I read there is such a sword in some museum, i just need to find a pic, but it means it is actually very doable...<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T16:16:29-07:00","id":1511486,"title":"","content":"Ultimately there's a problem where the Disciplines aren't realistic on top of the Attributes not being realistic, so trying to modify mundane weapons realistically to them doesn't really work.<br /> <br /> Can you make a bow that could take advantage of Strength 6/Potence 6 for draw strength with medieval tech? Yeah, probably. Is it a realistic weapon? That's much harder to answer. Bluecho brings up things like balance, but also things like size of the weapon, size of the individual missiles, etc. If you run things more &quot;fantasy/superpowers&quot; where you can lift a car with super-strength even though cars would just break from not being supported properly, you get a different answer than if you try to be more realistic about physics and just because you can lift two tons doesn't mean the two ton item can withstand being lifted by two human sized hands.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T16:28:59-07:00","id":1511487,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511482#post1511482\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sufficiently big and heavy weapons would throw off the wielder's balance with every swing or even just lifting it, regardless of how supernaturally strong they are. So whether it's melee or thrown, a weapon can only get so large on a human frame. You'd need to be physically larger (and thus heavier) for it to be practical. (Ex: A giant Bygone, like from <i>Bygone Bestiary</i>. But that raises unrelated complications.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, the loss of balance IS an issue of strength, so I don't quite agree with that assessment. My concern is about size because it's unwieldy moving around (a 3 meters long sword would require Dexterity checks to be moved without hitting something by mistake, more than being a Strength problem, and a siege ballista is hard to carry around and can't be pulled if the human arm length is too short for it (as it obviously is).<br /> It's the same reason why I don't bother wielding a halberd single-handedly: rules wise with Str 6 one could, and in my understanding the high Str is what would prevent loss of balance, but the size makes it inconvenient. If you wield it from the center of the shaft, like with a spear when you use it in one hand, a halberd is nothing more than an axe with a needlessly long shaft. If you wield it from the bottom of the shaft, being a slashing weapon, it's gonna be awkward because things will hit the shaft as you swing it.<br /> It's different with the spear because it's a thrusting weapon.<br /> <br /> Re: lead and tungsten: yes, I agree on that, though I wonder if that would make the weapon less sturdy. I don't actually know, what's sturdier: lead/tungsten or steel?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T16:51:50-07:00","id":1511488,"title":"","content":"Part of the problem with trying to modify real world weapons for supernatural interactions is that physics and terrain restrictions don't go away.<br /> <br /> In the case of a bow, it is less a question of using different materials and more a question of design. Look at even the smallest of the roman ballistas or scorpions, hand that to a kindred with a high strength+potence score and essentially they can free hand draw it, rather than using the crank systems. The problem is that those weapons are way too bulky to carry around with you. <br /> <br /> Melee weapons likewise gets complicated because the materials just won't hold up to the excessive forces being put on them. This isn't Exalted with magical material Daiklaives, anything of that size and weight would probably snap its handle after a couple battles and more of a problem the insane weight would mean the vampire is walking around with a couple hundred extra pounds at all times, the dark ages were not exactly known for their building codes, so figure on wooden stairs collapsing, bridge boards breaking, etc.<br /> <br /> Now the closest thing you could probably pull off is a quarter staff made out of solid iron. The modern day version of this would be a wrecking bar, the enhanced strength just means you could move it at the same speed as a wooden staff. The important thing is that since it is iron, if it gets bent, you just bend it back into the proper shape. While it is true that such a staff couldn't cut opponents, getting hit by such a thing would cave in plate armor like getting hit by a cannon ball.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T16:53:33-07:00","id":1511489,"title":"","content":"Lead vs. tungsten vs. steel is entirely dependent on what you're doing to it. Lead is the softest of them, so it will deform most easily. That can make it more durable since you can also bang it back into shape, and deformation can increase the trauma of a wound. But that malleability means it's less effective than steel against steel armor because it will deform on strong armor instead of penetrating and deforming in flesh.<br /> <br /> Realistically tungsten isn't great in the context of DA, because it would be obnoxiously rare, and tungsten metallurgy was still fairly limited. Without modern purification techniques, you'd have a metal like tempered glass: incredibly strong - better by most metrics than lead or steel - in one direction, and a light tap will shatter it to pieces from a different direction.<br /> <br /> Steel, though very dependent on how good your smith is with different types of steel, is in the middle of the two; one of the reason it's historically more popular in combat use besides lead being used for sling ammo. If you know your steel, it's much easier to get the right mixture of malleability and durability for whatever use you want to put it to. <br /> <br /> Metal is complicated because there's so many different ways to measure &quot;sturdy.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T18:00:25-07:00","id":1511494,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When dealing with characters with Strength + Potence 6+, is it still possible to produce bows with a draw weight suited to them, for more damage? </div> </div> </div>At that point you're just much better off with a magical weapon or access to magic material and a good mystic blacksmith or a fetish, or a ritual, or anything magic or something like that if you want more damage, heck! you can even use your own Potence (<i>Master of the Forge</i>) to create better/magic weapons if you don't want to go &quot;too wild&quot;. No need for &quot;giant weapons or giant bows&quot;. There's plenty of magic stuff in the WoD and it's not really that hard to find them. As one friend of mine, who is a fan of superheroes and D&amp;D (and naturally a total nerd like I am lol) told me some 20 years ago, the WoD is potentially more fantastic than D&amp;D and more &quot;over the top&quot; than Marvel superheroes if you really want it to be, so it all depends on the story and the Storyteller, etc.<br /> There are A LOT of things to make you martially more efficient without depending on the limitations of human craft (as you said you're playing in the Dark Ages).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","user":"Herr Meister","date":". 05-12-2023, 09:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T18:17:58-07:00","id":1511495,"title":"","content":"OK, I've done some more research.<br /> <br /> Medieval weapons weighed on average between 0.5 and 3 kg. That's including twohanded war hammers and axes.<br /> <br /> In VDA20, the heaviest weapons in the manual require Min Str 4 and two hands, or Min Str 6 and one hand. That would be our 3 kg weapons. So let's say that each dot of strength allows you to wield comfortably 0.5 kg of weapon, given the fact that you need to swing it potentially hundreds of time and keep balance. This is very different from the weight you can carry (up to 10 kg per dot of Str + Potence before your speed starts being affected) and the weight you can lift one off (which is a lot more, but I don't remember the table for it by heart) by the game's rules.<br /> <br /> This means that the heaviest weapon we may need, if we have a 4th generation with Str 9 and Potence 9, is 9 kg.<br /> <br /> Just thinking of dumbbells, barbells and kettlebells and the weights they are used for, it seems clear to me that designing a wooden or metal shaft able to manage 9 kg (shaft's weight included) should not be that crazy. It's obviously easier for shorter shafts, like those of war hammers and axes, than for long shafts like polearms, because the heavier weight on the extremity of the shaft of the polearm would increase the risk of breaking the shaft or bending it. It also makes balance harder, but I said in a previous comment that I understand that as covered by the user's Strength. So the main concern is structural, for the weapon itself.<br /> <br /> It's also clear that increasing the weight is only useful for slashing or blunt weapons: thrusting weapons like spears, for example, don't hurt more just because they're heavier (except maybe when thrown), unlike an axe or war hammer. With swords, since to increase the weight we need to &quot;fatten&quot; the blade, heavier blades will do more damage but will not be useful for thrusting, only slashing. In this, they become a bit similar to axes. There are, in the world, swords designed to have a broad and heavy blade that cuts on one side (I've seen a few from Asia but I don't know their proper names), for example, and that's what we would end up with unless we want to imitate the design of Cloud's sword from Final Fantasy 7.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, one can develop innovative designs suited to the extra weight: one option could be a cilindrical or spherical &quot;glove&quot; of metal weighing 9 kg with a cavity and handle inside, and maybe even some padding there to protect the hand and wrist from damage as they are inside the cilindrical or spherical glove. Punches from these would be pretty damaging, and they could be improved with spikes or blades to slash and thrust. The main disadvantage would be reach, though I don't think that's a big worry for a 4th generation vampire.<br /> Another is a form like a disk with a small round shield on it to add weight and protection and broad blades that come out of it, say on two or three sides. I have in mind some werewolf miniature from the game Confrontation or the Demon Hunter's weapon in Warcraft 3, or a spherical glove surrounded by a circular blade. It would require developing a special fighting style, but it would be doable. Again, one loses reach, but gains other things, including the equivalent of one or two small shields (which in VDA20 can parry at diff.4) whilst wielding a weapon.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bThis is what's working in my imagination.<br /> <br /> As for bows, bigger ones are obviously not much of an option. Draw weight, which is the measure of how hard it is to pull the string and arms of the bow, from which the strength of the arrow's impact depends, can be increased in an ancient composite bow by adding layers of more rigid (but still elastic) materials and using a string weaved from tendon and similar strong materials, without altering the size of the bow. One may also change the curvature of the bow's arms for extra draw weight.<br /> Let's also keep in mind, like with the weight of melee weapons, that it's not enough to be able to pull the bow once, but that it needs to be pulled hundreds of times. Thus, the actual draw weight of historical bows does not need to be the maximum achievable: they stopped designing heavier bows when there was nobody strong enough to wield them in combat, but the materials and techniques might still have untapped potential.<br /> <br /> <br /> My difficulty is in that I don't know how far one can go with that before the materials and techniques are not adequate anymore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T18:39:09-07:00","id":1511496,"title":"","content":"Apparently the heaviest draw weight used by the strongest English long bowmen was 200 lbs. Let's assume that's our value of reference for Str 5. 200 / 5 = 40 lbs per dot of Str + Potence. Our 4th gen vampire with 9 in both needs 40 x 18 = 720 lbs draw weight. The heaviest draw weight I heard of, but it may be legendary, is 496 lbs for Guru Gobind Singh Ji's bow. After that, it's the 200 used as reference above.(source: <a href=\"https://weightofstuff.com/heaviest-bow-draw-weight-ever/\" target=\"_blank\">https://weightofstuff.com/heaviest-b...w-weight-ever/</a>)<br /> <br /> I need to see if there are ways to raise the weight to 720 lbs without making the bow bigger or too big.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T19:03:05-07:00","id":1511499,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511494#post1511494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> At that point you're just much better off with a magical weapon or access to magic material and a good mystic blacksmith or a fetish, or a ritual, or anything magic or something like that if you want more damage, heck! you can even use your own Potence (<i>Master of the Forge</i>) to create better/magic weapons if you don't want to go &quot;too wild&quot;. No need for &quot;giant weapons or giant bows&quot;. There's plenty of magic stuff in the WoD and it's not really that hard to find them. As one friend of mine, who is a fan of superheroes and D&amp;D (and naturally a total nerd like I am lol) told me some 20 years ago, the WoD is potentially more fantastic than D&amp;D and more &quot;over the top&quot; than Marvel superheroes if you really want it to be, so it all depends on the story and the Storyteller, etc.<br /> There are A LOT of things to make you martially more efficient without depending on the limitations of human craft (as you said you're playing in the Dark Ages).</div> </div> </div> </div>I get your point, but to be clear, I am trying to <i>avoid</i> giant weapons, not use them. I am trying to make weapons customised for individual strength, including supernatural, without making goofish ugly giant weapons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T19:09:51-07:00","id":1511500,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511499#post1511499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I get your point, but to be clear, I am trying to <i>avoid</i> giant weapons, not use them. I am trying to make weapons customised for individual strength, including supernatural, without making goofish ugly giant weapons.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then you don't need &quot;goofish ugly giant weapons&quot;, you can get a weapon that deals aggravated damage (quite easily for an elder) and/or you can make your own with Potence powers like <i>Master of the Forge</i>, that also increases the damage of any weapon if you score a good amount of successes. As I said, there are many ways to have better weapons to deal more damage, from the easiest ones to the most absurds/fantastics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T19:36:26-07:00","id":1511502,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511500#post1511500\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Then you don't need &quot;goofish ugly giant weapons&quot;, you can get a weapon that deals aggravated damage (quite easily for an elder) and/or you can make your own with Potence powers like <i>Master of the Forge</i>, that also increases the damage of any weapon if you score a good amount of successes. As I said, there are many ways to have better weapons to deal more damage, from the easiest ones to the most absurds/fantastics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I can use those things, but there is no reason why I shouldn't investigate a simple one like this.<br /> <br /> Matter of fact, if you read the other comments I wrote, I have already solved the issue as far as melee weapons are concerned: the heaviest weapon a vampire with Str 9 and Potence 9 can wield in one hand without penalties is 9 kg (ignoring odd powers or single instances of smashing a car into a person, which is not something one does 100s of times like swinging a sword) if one goes by proportion with the weight of real medieval weapons. Making 9kg weapons is not impossible (I addressed this in detail in another comment), and 9 kg is basically a kettlebell. Literally, a kettlebell with spikes and/or a circular blade is a non-magical weapon fit for a Brujah Methuselah. If one also has access to other powers, all the better. This can be done however without spending xp or asking favours from other supernaturals, you just show up at your local blacksmith and it's probably gonna be ready the following night. Not useless.<br /> <br /> EDIT: apologies. In hindsight it seems this message was worded unnecessarily strongly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":". 05-12-2023, 11:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-13T04:07:58-07:00","id":1511534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511496#post1511496\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Apparently the heaviest draw weight used by the strongest English long bowmen was 200 lbs. Let's assume that's our value of reference for Str 5. 200 / 5 = 40 lbs per dot of Str + Potence. Our 4th gen vampire with 9 in both needs 40 x 18 = 720 lbs draw weight. The heaviest draw weight I heard of, but it may be legendary, is 496 lbs for Guru Gobind Singh Ji's bow. After that, it's the 200 used as reference above.(source: <a href=\"https://weightofstuff.com/heaviest-bow-draw-weight-ever/\" target=\"_blank\">https://weightofstuff.com/heaviest-b...w-weight-ever/</a>)<br /> <br /> I need to see if there are ways to raise the weight to 720 lbs without making the bow bigger or too big.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Did more research. Medieval hand-held crossbows reached a draw weight of 1500 lbs. The difference with bows is the size (crossbows have shorter limbs, which increases the draw weight) and the mechanism (crossbows are pulled through a mechanism that makes it possible for a human to draw that much draw weight, though that makes it slower to reload). Since neither is a concern here, because the vampire would have the strength to pull by hand a crossbow instead of using the mechanism, a customised bow for supernatural strength would be smaller than normal bows but entirely possible and proportionately more damaging.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-13T05:36:01-07:00","id":1511538,"title":"","content":"You're taking one part of the physics here and not really factoring in lots of other things. You can't just take the steel prod off a crossbow and turn it into a short bow with the same draw weight. The stock plays an important part in keeping the whole thing from snapping under the stress and providing leverage. Keep in mind that the center of a bow, even a steel bow, is the weakest point because a human hand needs to comfortably grip it. The more draw weight you put on the arms, the more stress you're putting on the joint between the arm and the grip. Eventually either just trying to draw to maximum, or over multiple uses, there's a maximum amount of draw weight you can apply before the whole things falls apart. Crossbows, by keeping the prods on the stock, can have much stronger joints and thus withstand much higher draw weights since they never have to narrow for a grip.<br /> <br /> Steel is also a less effective energy medium than wood for bows. A steel bow transfers less energy to the missile than a wood one does; a 150 lb wooden long bow isn't actually ten times weaker than a 1,500 lb steel crossbow. Depending on the specifics of design and materials, the actual joules the missile has when loosed could actually be even because there's that much of a difference in energy loss when you store potential energy in a steel spring like that. That energy is also lost as heat. If a supernaturally strong vampire tries to use a high draw weight crossbow and just hand pull it to fire as rapidly as a standard wooden bow, the arms are going to start heating up fast. That heat will ruin the temper on the steel, and ruin it's ability to function at all.<br /> <br /> The big reason to go with the crossbow for normal humans is because very few people are fit enough to pull such a large draw, hold it to aim, and then do it again, and again, and again. Crossbows hold the weight for the marksman, and a crank/winch is slower (though that also means no overheating the metal) but less maximum output to draw it.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of factors at play when talking about trying to push the extremes of even what feels like such simple technology.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-13T05:56:29-07:00","id":1511539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511538#post1511538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're taking one part of the physics here and not really factoring in lots of other things. You can't just take the steel prod off a crossbow and turn it into a short bow with the same draw weight. The stock plays an important part in keeping the whole thing from snapping under the stress and providing leverage. Keep in mind that the center of a bow, even a steel bow, is the weakest point because a human hand needs to comfortably grip it. The more draw weight you put on the arms, the more stress you're putting on the joint between the arm and the grip. Eventually either just trying to draw to maximum, or over multiple uses, there's a maximum amount of draw weight you can apply before the whole things falls apart. Crossbows, by keeping the prods on the stock, can have much stronger joints and thus withstand much higher draw weights since they never have to narrow for a grip.<br /> <br /> Steel is also a less effective energy medium than wood for bows. A steel bow transfers less energy to the missile than a wood one does; a 150 lb wooden long bow isn't actually ten times weaker than a 1,500 lb steel crossbow. Depending on the specifics of design and materials, the actual joules the missile has when loosed could actually be even because there's that much of a difference in energy loss when you store potential energy in a steel spring like that. That energy is also lost as heat. If a supernaturally strong vampire tries to use a high draw weight crossbow and just hand pull it to fire as rapidly as a standard wooden bow, the arms are going to start heating up fast. That heat will ruin the temper on the steel, and ruin it's ability to function at all.<br /> <br /> The big reason to go with the crossbow for normal humans is because very few people are fit enough to pull such a large draw, hold it to aim, and then do it again, and again, and again. Crossbows hold the weight for the marksman, and a crank/winch is slower (though that also means no overheating the metal) but less maximum output to draw it.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of factors at play when talking about trying to push the extremes of even what feels like such simple technology.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. So, is it impossible to produce a bow or similar weapon with a draw weight of 720 lbs (I'm not really looking for more than that) with medieval technology? Is there no wood, string material and size that combined would produce that effect? Couldn't the handle issue between bow and crossbow be solved in some other way, for example by loading and releasing a crossbow by free hand (if one has enough strength,as in this case), like with a bow, but keeping the stock to avoid the snapping issue? This to ensure stronger and yet more frequent shooting? The strength of a crossbow, and the speed of an archer.<br /> <br /> I am also reading that a crossbow, having more draw weight but less draw length than a bow, doesn't actually have much more strength in terms of the damage of the impact.<br /> <br /> And I am starting to wonder also if increasing the strength required for the bow, even if it leads to faster and more impactful projectiles, would actually mean more <i>dice of damage</i> in game terms, rather than just a greater range. After all, I assume a bullet penetrates faster and more harmfully than an arrow, and yet it does not inflict tons of damage in the game, unless it hits vital points. Putting a hole in your enemy, at the end of the day, only does a certain amount of damage, and most of it depends on where it hits. Larger projectiles would change this, but that would mean going toward those giant weapons I am avoiding because goofy and impractical.<br /> <br /> I may have to scrap the ideas on the customised bow and let my character stick to thrown weapons, which already benefit from increasing character strength. Perhaps one can design heavier and larger hatchets for extra damage, after working out the required balance between the various parts of the weapon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":". 05-13-2023, 09:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-13T11:50:22-07:00","id":1511557,"title":"","content":"What year is it even? what's considered the medieval period lasts around a thousand years, and a good amount of progress was made in this time. <br /> <br /> From a <b>gameplay mechanic standpoint</b>, I'm hesitant about giving any mundane melee weapon Strength+5 damage. If anyone wants to use a 100kg maul, I'd argue they' be wasting strength trying to handle the thing and a 10kg maul would be what peak performance looks like. I was arguing with myself if using <i>ward against vampires</i> on a weapon should add to the weapon's damage or be resolved in a separate roll. Maybe the former for <i>Ward</i> but for a lot of magic I'd use the later. On a macro level, if mundane attributes and abilities peak at 5, perhaps melee weapons should too, although I wouldn't hold armour to such a standard simply because thickening steel does indeed scale really well. <br /> <br /> For non-meleeweapons, like, IRL humans have made some fucking terrifying stuff. Weapons made for use against concrete and steel create haunting results when used on flesh. Garand thumb released a video where a support vehicle, Tank and Howitzer shot gelatine dummies: The first is closed-casket stuff but the latter two burst the human aproximate like it was a water balloon. With that in mind.. the system has never really been keen on simulating high damage weaponry. Anything beyond 8 dice is an inconsistent land. It could do it I think, but things like 30mm canons usually have rules like -big but not crazy damage, half soak values- <br /> <br /> For non-gunpowder projectile weapons, nothing has that destructive power. <br /> <br /> If you want a more simulationist game, a lot of rifles will do more than 8 damage. the joules needed for every dice after the 8th increase exponentially. I calculated that WW1 rifle rounds would do between 10 and 12 damage, which makes sense given that they were using what can be referred to as &quot;full sized&quot; rounds optimized to kill distant human targets in a single hit. Given characters have seven health levels, assume half the dice become damage, and add a few dice due to degrees of success, and that works out excellently. But when it comes to bow-type weapons... there's nothing with anywhere near that force. Strongest modern crossbow is comparable to an intermediate round as I said earlier. Crossbows back then had similar strengths but with higher poundages because the technology was far less effecient. <br /> <br /> For throwing weapons, I'd use that +5 rule I'm using for melee weapons. In DAV20, there's some armour ignoring weapons, I'd count the armour ignore in that +5 limit. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>From a narrative perspective</b></span><br /> I don't think vampires are going to appreciate someone swinging a symbolically big hammer around. Elders want peace and stability. Someone going out of their way to be an unstoppable badass, even if it's a sheriff, is a threat Massive weapons don't blend in well, and the silence of the blood must also be observed. Similarly, ignoring shitty game mechanics, this is the main reason vampires wouldn't wear a lot of armour even though they, not needing to breath, worry about exhaustion, and having super strength, should be able to comfortably wear and conceal more than ordinary humans could.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-13T15:36:08-07:00","id":1511570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511539#post1511539\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, is it impossible to produce a bow or similar weapon with a draw weight of 720 lbs (I'm not really looking for more than that) with medieval technology?</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably possible, but you're getting into the &quot;obviously designed for an inhuman monster or shonen anime protagonist&quot; territory of what it would look like.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Couldn't the handle issue between bow and crossbow be solved in some other way, for example by loading and releasing a crossbow by free hand (if one has enough strength,as in this case), like with a bow, but keeping the stock to avoid the snapping issue? This to ensure stronger and yet more frequent shooting? The strength of a crossbow, and the speed of an archer. </div> </div> </div>Again there's problems that arise when you start pushing these boundaries. If you make a crossbow like this, you're invariably sacrificing accuracy to keep up that speed. A normal bow has a strong advantage because you can keep the weapon aligned with a target while knocking and firing fresh arrows. A crossbow requires you to adjust the position of the weapon to pull the string, meaning you lose your sighting each time. So you can make a weapon that fires as fast a a bow, with the strength of a crossbow, but you'd be sacrificing a huge amount of accuracy in the process. There is such a weapon that was used by the Song Dynasty in China, but it was explicitly a mass volley weapon where the archers would fire laying on their backs with their feet bracing the bow. As you can imagine, with a few hundred archers they could volley lots and lots of arrows, but nobody was accurately picking off specific targets like that.<br /> <br /> There's just a basic issue of physics were no matter how strong you are, you can't hold a crossbow (or rifle) stock with one hand and keep the weapon aligned with your sighting mechanism. The distant end of the weapon is going to move around. You have good instincts to think about range increments instead of other aspects of the weapon here, because when the end of that crossbow is shifting around a few centimeters it doesn't really matter at a few meters, but it means you're not hitting whatever you're aiming at 50 meters away as the deflection compounds with distance from the target.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> After all, I assume a bullet penetrates faster and more harmfully than an arrow, and yet it does not inflict tons of damage in the game, unless it hits vital points. </div> </div> </div>This is again... complicated. The bullet from a firearm is definitely faster than an arrow, but it's also a lot smaller. It's entirely possible for a bullet to blast right through a target and just miss any major organ or blood vessel. A broad head arrow on the other hand, due to its size, is vastly more likely to cause a hemorrhage or a gut perforation without having to accurately aim for a specific part of the body.<br /> <br /> That's why modern bullets are meant to deform in the target's body: they flatten, growing larger in surface area so they hit more stuff as they go, and tumble around more, to maximize the odds of causing lethal wounds.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I may have to scrap the ideas on the customised bow and let my character stick to thrown weapons, which already benefit from increasing character strength. </div> </div> </div>The Francisca is probably a good place to start. They were a well known design in medieval Europe, and the Franks were notorious for using them to shred enemy shield walls before closing into a melee. Some designs were large enough to be a fighting axe as well as a throwing axe, so they scale up fairly large without losing their primary function.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-14T13:51:02-07:00","id":1511647,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically?p=1511557#post1511557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What year is it even? what's considered the medieval period lasts around a thousand years, and a good amount of progress was made in this time.<br /> <br /> From a <b>gameplay mechanic standpoint</b>, I'm hesitant about giving any mundane melee weapon Strength+5 damage. If anyone wants to use a 100kg maul, I'd argue they' be wasting strength trying to handle the thing and a 10kg maul would be what peak performance looks like. I was arguing with myself if using <i>ward against vampires</i> on a weapon should add to the weapon's damage or be resolved in a separate roll. Maybe the former for <i>Ward</i> but for a lot of magic I'd use the later. On a macro level, if mundane attributes and abilities peak at 5, perhaps melee weapons should too, although I wouldn't hold armour to such a standard simply because thickening steel does indeed scale really well.<br /> <br /> For non-meleeweapons, like, IRL humans have made some fucking terrifying stuff. Weapons made for use against concrete and steel create haunting results when used on flesh. Garand thumb released a video where a support vehicle, Tank and Howitzer shot gelatine dummies: The first is closed-casket stuff but the latter two burst the human aproximate like it was a water balloon. With that in mind.. the system has never really been keen on simulating high damage weaponry. Anything beyond 8 dice is an inconsistent land. It could do it I think, but things like 30mm canons usually have rules like -big but not crazy damage, half soak values-<br /> <br /> For non-gunpowder projectile weapons, nothing has that destructive power.<br /> <br /> If you want a more simulationist game, a lot of rifles will do more than 8 damage. the joules needed for every dice after the 8th increase exponentially. I calculated that WW1 rifle rounds would do between 10 and 12 damage, which makes sense given that they were using what can be referred to as &quot;full sized&quot; rounds optimized to kill distant human targets in a single hit. Given characters have seven health levels, assume half the dice become damage, and add a few dice due to degrees of success, and that works out excellently. But when it comes to bow-type weapons... there's nothing with anywhere near that force. Strongest modern crossbow is comparable to an intermediate round as I said earlier. Crossbows back then had similar strengths but with higher poundages because the technology was far less effecient.<br /> <br /> For throwing weapons, I'd use that +5 rule I'm using for melee weapons. In DAV20, there's some armour ignoring weapons, I'd count the armour ignore in that +5 limit.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>From a narrative perspective</b></span><br /> I don't think vampires are going to appreciate someone swinging a symbolically big hammer around. Elders want peace and stability. Someone going out of their way to be an unstoppable badass, even if it's a sheriff, is a threat Massive weapons don't blend in well, and the silence of the blood must also be observed. Similarly, ignoring shitty game mechanics, this is the main reason vampires wouldn't wear a lot of armour even though they, not needing to breath, worry about exhaustion, and having super strength, should be able to comfortably wear and conceal more than ordinary humans could.</div> </div> </div> </div>Year: 1105 if memory serves me well.<br /> <br /> 100 / 10 kg maul: as explained in another comment, all the weapons that require Str 4 two-handed or 6 one-handed weighed about 3 kg max historically. Sure, a strong person is able to lift much more, but swinging a weapon in all directions for hours whilst wearing up to 25 kg of armour is another thing. So my take is that each point of Str+Potence allows you to wield +0.5 kg of weapon without penalty, and that adds +1 to the weapon's damage (I mean, from the base line of +4 for Str 4 two-handed / Str 6 one-handed, so one-handed it's 2 dice of the damage less than the one-handed Min Str) if the weapon makes use of weight significantly (a great sword, war hammer or halberd do; a spear or stock for thrusting not really). As for how elders would react, extra heavy weapons beneath 9 kg have existed and are found in museums. They were either ceremonial or for practice (practicing on a heavier weapon means being facilitated when using the normal weapon in battle, basically like Goku training with extra weights and taking them off when fighting seriously). So, honestly, a guy going around with a 4 kg weapon rather than the conventional 3 kg would be <i>that </i>noticeable. He would seem eccentric at best. Someone might even underestimate him, thinking he has not realised such a heavy weapon would be inefficient in real battle. Pity for them, it is very efficient for he who has Potence and/or a low generation. A 9 kg weapon would be surely noticeable (the blade would be much broader than normal, or longer, or thicker, or all together depending on design) but, again, the fighter might get some fame for it without people thinking he's an actual supernatural creature yet, unless they actually see him wield it for 3 hours non-stop in battle without tiring. He will just pass for a cocky, inexperienced yet freakishly strong fighter.<br /> <br /> Honestly, if I get to ST sometime I'm probably gonna have it as a normal thing for supernaturals to get extra heavy weapons and armour crafted, as long as they can pass it as a personal eccentric thing instead of being immediately identified as supernatural. It would be a nice niche business for a vampire or ghoul blacksmith.<br /> <br /> +5 as limit: from a game mechanics and balance standpoint, that's a reasonable choice.<br /> <br /> Modern weapons: I haven't thought much about that, but those seem good considerations","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-14T13:51:51-07:00","id":1511649,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511471-dark-ages-customising-weapons-to-superhuman-strength-realistically#post1511471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know that in the real world one can - and would, if professional - customise the draw weight of their bow to match their strength.<br /> When dealing with characters with Strength + Potence 6+, is it still possible to produce bows with a draw weight suited to them, for more damage?<br /> In a dark age setting, would there be materials available to make composite bows for them? I have been told that the shorter the arms of the bow, the greater the draw weight, so size would not seem to be a problem for superhuman bows.</div> </div> </div> </div>I material wise is should work, I mean they made some pretty big siege engines\u200b and crossbows, that were powered by bows, i think this one is good example, problem is likely the weight and size of that bow and the arrows you would need for it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AA5M0QKXtWU\" data-code=\"AA5M0QKXtWU\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/AA5M0QKXtWU/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}